import VersoManual
import VersoBlueprint
import TDCSG.CriticalRadius.Five

open Verso.Genre Verso.Genre.Manual Informal

#doc (Manual) "Compound symmetry" =>

%%%
tag := "compound-symmetry"
%%%

The main theorem is a statement about one family of groups: the groups generated by turning two
overlapping disks in the plane. This chapter sets up the general notion these groups belong to,
a compound symmetry group of a family of balls, exactly as the Lean library defines it, and proves
the general facts about it that the later chapters use. Chapter 2 specializes it to two disks and
states the main theorem.

The reader is assumed to know groups, metric spaces and isometries, but not Lean. Each Lean object
used below is introduced with a plain explanation, a guide to its notation, and its exact name in
Mathlib, the mathematical library of Lean, linked to the Mathlib documentation. A Lean panel
beside each numbered statement shows the declarations that the statement describes.

*The ingredients from Mathlib.* Throughout, $`X` is a pseudometric space: a set with a distance function
$`d(x,y)\ge0` that is symmetric, vanishes on the diagonal and satisfies the triangle inequality.
Distinct points may be at distance zero. Lean calls this a `PseudoMetricSpace`; nothing below
needs more, and every metric space is one.

* *Permutations.* A permutation of $`X` is a bijection $`X\to X`. Lean stores it together with
  its inverse, as an element of
  [`Equiv.Perm X`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Logic/Equiv/Defs.html#Equiv.Perm),
  written `Perm X` once the namespace `Equiv` is open. These permutations form a group: the
  product `σ * τ` is the composite that applies `τ` first, so `(σ * τ) x = σ (τ x)`, and `1` is the
  identity. A permutation `σ` is applied to a point `x` by writing `σ x`.

* *Subgroups.* For a group `G`, a subgroup `K : Subgroup G`, an element of
  [`Subgroup G`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Group/Subgroup/Defs.html#Subgroup),
  is a subset of `G` that contains `1` and is closed under products and inverses. Membership is
  written `g ∈ K`. A subgroup is also used as a group in its own right, whose elements are the
  members of `K`; this is how `Finite K` and `Nat.card K` below are to be read.

* *Generated subgroups.* For a subset `S` of `G`,
  [`Subgroup.closure S`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Group/Subgroup/Lattice.html#Subgroup.closure)
  is the subgroup generated by `S`, the smallest subgroup containing `S`. Its elements are the
  finite products of elements of `S` and their inverses.

* *Suprema of subgroups.* The subgroups of `G`, ordered by inclusion, form a complete lattice
  ([`Subgroup.instCompleteLattice`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Group/Subgroup/Lattice.html#Subgroup.instCompleteLattice)).
  For a family `K i` of subgroups indexed by `i : ι`, the supremum `⨆ i, K i`, an instance of
  [`iSup`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Order/SetNotation.html#iSup),
  is the smallest subgroup containing every `K i`, that is, the subgroup generated by their union
  ([`Subgroup.iSup_eq_closure`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Group/Subgroup/Lattice.html#Subgroup.iSup_eq_closure)).
  The supremum of the empty family is the trivial subgroup.

* *Cyclic subgroups.* For `g : G`,
  [`Subgroup.zpowers g`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Group/Subgroup/ZPowers/Basic.html#Subgroup.zpowers)
  is the subgroup of the integer powers $`g^k`, $`k\in\mathbb Z`, which is the subgroup
  generated by $`g`.

* *Isometries.* For pseudometric spaces `α` and `β`, an element of `α ≃ᵢ β`, notation for
  [`IsometryEquiv α β`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/Isometry.html#IsometryEquiv),
  is a bijection from `α` onto `β` that preserves distances. The isometries of `α` onto itself,
  `α ≃ᵢ α`, form a group under composition.

* *Closed balls.* For a center `c : X` and a radius `r : ℝ`,
  [`Metric.closedBall c r`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/Pseudo/Defs.html#Metric.closedBall)
  is the set $`\{x\in X : d(x,c)\le r\}`. The radius is an arbitrary real number; for $`r<0` the
  ball is empty
  ([`Metric.closedBall_eq_empty`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/Pseudo/Defs.html#Metric.closedBall_eq_empty)).
  Lean also uses a set as a space whose points are the members of the set, with the restricted
  distance. So `closedBall c r ≃ᵢ closedBall c r` is the group of isometries of the ball, regarded
  as a pseudometric space in its own right.

* *Homomorphisms.* For groups `G` and `H`, an element `f : G →* H` of
  [`MonoidHom G H`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Group/Hom/Defs.html#MonoidHom)
  is a group homomorphism: a map with `f 1 = 1` and `f (g * h) = f g * f h`. For a subgroup
  `K` of `G`, `K.map f`, that is
  [`Subgroup.map`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Group/Subgroup/Map.html#Subgroup.map),
  is the image subgroup $`f(K)`. An isomorphism of groups is written `G ≃* H`
  ([`MulEquiv`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Group/Equiv/Defs.html#MulEquiv)).

* *Orbits.* A subgroup `G` of `Perm X` acts on `X` by $`g\cdot x=g(x)`.
  [`MulAction.orbit G x`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/GroupTheory/GroupAction/Defs.html#MulAction.orbit)
  is the orbit $`\{g(x) : g\in G\}` of the point `x`. Its size is measured by
  [`Set.encard`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Data/Set/Card.html#Set.encard),
  the number of elements of a set as a value in $`\{0,1,2,\ldots\}\cup\{\infty\}`.

* *Finiteness and order.* For a group `G`,
  [`Group.FG G`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/GroupTheory/Finiteness.html#Group.FG)
  says that `G` is generated by a finite set, and
  [`Nat.card G`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/SetTheory/Cardinal/Finite.html#Nat.card)
  is its order. By convention `Nat.card` is $`0` on an infinite type.

*The definition.* Fix a pseudometric space $`X`, an index set $`\iota`, and for each $`i\in\iota` a center
$`c_i\in X` and a radius $`r_i\in\mathbb R`. Write $`B_i` for the closed ball of radius $`r_i`
about $`c_i`, and $`U=\bigcup_i B_i` for the union of the balls. For each $`i` choose a group
$`H_i` of isometries of $`B_i` onto itself.

:::definition "ball_extension" (lean := "IsometryEquiv.toPerm, TDCSG.ballPerm")
Let $`B` be the closed ball of radius $`r` about $`c` in $`X`. An isometry $`h` of $`B` onto
itself extends to the permutation $`\hat h` of $`X` that agrees with $`h` on $`B` and fixes every
point outside $`B`. The extension $`h\mapsto\hat h` is a group homomorphism from the isometry group
of $`B` to the permutations of $`X`.
:::

In Lean the extension is `TDCSG.ballPerm c r`, of type
`(closedBall c r ≃ᵢ closedBall c r) →* Perm X`. It is the composite of two maps:
`IsometryEquiv.toPerm`, which forgets that an isometry of the ball preserves distances and keeps
only the underlying permutation of the ball, and
[`Equiv.Perm.ofSubtype`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Group/End.html#Equiv.Perm.ofSubtype),
which extends a permutation of a subset to the whole space by the identity.

:::lemma_ "ball_extension_facts" (lean := "TDCSG.ballPerm_apply_of_mem, TDCSG.ballPerm_apply_of_notMem, TDCSG.ballPerm_injective, TDCSG.ballPerm_eq_one_of_neg") (uses := "ball_extension")
The extension $`\hat h` acts on $`B` as $`h` and fixes every point outside $`B`. The extension
homomorphism is injective. If $`r<0`, then $`\hat h` is the identity.
:::

:::proof "ball_extension_facts" (lean := "IsometryEquiv.toPerm_injective")
The first two statements are the defining property of the extension by the identity. An isometry
of $`B` is determined by its underlying map, and a permutation of $`B` is recovered from its
extension by restricting to $`B`, so both maps in the composite are injective. For $`r<0` the
ball is empty, so every point lies outside it and is fixed.
:::

:::definition "ball_restriction" (lean := "TDCSG.ballRestrict")
Let $`e` be an isometry of $`X` onto itself with $`e(c)=c`. Its restriction to the closed ball
of radius $`r` about $`c` is an isometry of the ball onto itself, because
$`d(e(x),c)=d(e(x),e(c))=d(x,c)`.
:::

:::definition "compound_group" (lean := "TDCSG.ballUnion, TDCSG.compoundSymmetryGroup") (uses := "ball_extension")
The compound symmetry group of the balls $`B_i` with the isometry groups $`H_i` is the subgroup
of the permutations of $`X` generated by the extensions $`\hat h` of all $`h\in H_i`, for all
$`i\in\iota`. The union of the balls is $`U=\bigcup_i B_i`.
:::

The Lean definition reads
`compoundSymmetryGroup c r H = ⨆ i, (H i).map (ballPerm (c i) (r i))`. Here `c : ι → X` and
`r : ι → ℝ` list the centers and radii, `H i` is a subgroup of
`closedBall (c i) (r i) ≃ᵢ closedBall (c i) (r i)`, its image `(H i).map (ballPerm (c i) (r i))`
under the extension homomorphism is a subgroup of `Perm X`, and `⨆ i` takes the subgroup
generated by all these images. The result is a `Subgroup (Perm X)`.

*Why permutations and not isometries.* A rotation of one disk in the plane, extended by the
identity, is not an isometry of the plane, and it is not even continuous: points on the boundary
circle move, while points just outside it stay fixed. So the extensions do not lie in the
isometry group of $`X`, and the only common home for all of them is the group of all bijections
of $`X`. The metric is used only to say what the balls are and which maps of a ball are allowed.

*Empty balls and empty families.* Radii are arbitrary real numbers. A ball of negative radius is
empty, its only isometry is the identity, and its extension is the identity of $`X`, so it
contributes nothing to the group. With no balls at all, $`\iota` empty, the supremum of the empty
family is the trivial group. These conventions make the definition total, with no hypotheses on
the radii.

*Generators.* The definition takes whole groups $`H_i`, but the group is generated by the
extensions of generators of the $`H_i`. In particular, when each $`H_i` is the cyclic group of one
isometry $`h_i`, the compound symmetry group is generated by the extensions $`\hat h_i`. For two
disks this is how the definition meets the usual description, the group generated by the two
disk rotations (Chapter 2).

:::lemma_ "compound_generators" (lean := "TDCSG.compoundSymmetryGroup_le_iff, TDCSG.compoundSymmetryGroup_eq_closure_of_eq_closure, TDCSG.compoundSymmetryGroup_zpowers") (uses := "compound_group")
A subgroup $`K` of the permutations of $`X` contains the compound symmetry group if and only if
it contains $`\hat h` for every $`h\in H_i` and every $`i`. If each $`H_i` is generated by a set
$`S_i`, the compound symmetry group is generated by the extensions of the elements of all the
$`S_i`. If each $`H_i` is the cyclic group generated by $`h_i`, it is generated by the
extensions $`\hat h_i`.
:::

:::proof "compound_generators"
The supremum of the image subgroups is contained in $`K` exactly when each image is. The image of
a generated subgroup under a homomorphism is generated by the image of the generating set
([`MonoidHom.map_closure`](https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Algebra/Group/Subgroup/Map.html#MonoidHom.map_closure)),
and the supremum of generated subgroups is generated by the union of the generating sets. The
cyclic case takes $`S_i=\{h_i\}`.
:::

*General facts.* The following facts hold for every compound symmetry group. The later chapters use them for two
disks: the first two say that all motion happens inside the balls, the third computes the group
when the balls are disjoint, and the last two reduce finiteness of the group to a uniform bound
on its orbits, which is how the finite half of the main theorem is proved.

:::lemma_ "compound_support" (lean := "TDCSG.apply_eq_of_notMem_ballUnion, TDCSG.apply_mem_ballUnion_iff, TDCSG.image_ballUnion") (uses := "compound_generators, ball_extension_facts")
Every element $`g` of the compound symmetry group fixes each point outside $`U`, and maps $`U`
onto itself.
:::

:::proof "compound_support" (lean := "TDCSG.compoundSymmetryGroup_le_fixingSubgroup")
The permutations that fix every point outside $`U` form a subgroup. It contains every generator
$`\hat h`, which fixes every point outside its own ball, so it contains the whole group. If $`x\in
U` but $`g(x)\notin U`, then $`g` fixes $`g(x)`, so $`g(g(x))=g(x)`, and injectivity gives
$`g(x)=x\in U`, a contradiction. Conversely, a point outside $`U` is fixed. Applying this to
$`g^{-1}` as well shows that $`g` maps $`U` onto $`U`.
:::

:::lemma_ "compound_orbits" (lean := "TDCSG.orbit_subset_insert_ballUnion, TDCSG.orbit_eq_singleton_of_notMem_ballUnion") (uses := "compound_support")
The orbit of a point $`x` under the compound symmetry group lies in $`U\cup\{x\}`. If
$`x\notin U`, the orbit is $`\{x\}`.
:::

:::proof "compound_orbits"
If $`x\in U`, every $`g(x)` lies in $`U`. If $`x\notin U`, every $`g(x)` equals $`x`.
:::

:::theorem "compound_disjoint" (lean := "TDCSG.mulEquivPi, TDCSG.nonempty_mulEquiv_pi, TDCSG.natCard_compoundSymmetryGroup") (uses := "compound_group, ball_extension_facts")
Suppose $`\iota` is finite and the balls $`B_i` are pairwise disjoint. Then the compound symmetry
group is isomorphic to the product $`\prod_i H_i`; the inverse isomorphism sends a family
$`(h_i)` to the product of the extensions $`\hat h_i`. In particular its order is
$`\prod_i |H_i|`, with the convention of `Nat.card` that an infinite group has order $`0`.
:::

:::proof "compound_disjoint" (lean := "TDCSG.commute_ballPerm, TDCSG.iSupIndep_map_ballPerm, TDCSG.piToCompoundSymmetryGroup")
Extensions from two disjoint balls move disjoint sets of points, so they commute. Hence
multiplying the extensions of the components is a homomorphism from $`\prod_i H_i` to the
permutations of $`X`, and its image is the compound symmetry group. It is injective: on $`B_i`
the product acts as $`\hat h_i`, because the other factors fix $`B_i`, so a product equal to
the identity has every $`\hat h_i` trivial, and then every $`h_i` trivial by injectivity of the
extension.
:::

:::lemma_ "uniform_orbits" (lean := "MulAction.finite_of_encard_orbit_le")
A finitely generated group acting faithfully on a set is finite if the sizes of all point
orbits share one finite bound.
:::

:::proof "uniform_orbits" (lean := "MonoidHom.finite_of_fg")
Number the points of an orbit $`O` as $`1,\ldots,|O|`. The action on $`O` is then a homomorphism
to the symmetric group on $`|O|` letters, determined by the images of the generators. With
$`|O|` bounded there are only finitely many such homomorphisms. Map the group to the product
of the symmetric groups indexed by the homomorphisms that occur. An element of the kernel fixes
every point, so it is the identity by faithfulness.
:::

:::theorem "compound_finite" (lean := "TDCSG.instFGSubtypePermMemSubgroupCompoundSymmetryGroupOfFiniteOfIsometryEquivElemClosedBall, TDCSG.finite_compoundSymmetryGroup_of_encard_orbit_le") (uses := "compound_generators, uniform_orbits")
If $`\iota` is finite and every $`H_i` is finitely generated, the compound symmetry group is
finitely generated. A finitely generated compound symmetry group whose orbits all have at most
$`N` points, for one natural number $`N`, is finite.
:::

:::proof "compound_finite"
The extensions of finite generating sets of the finitely many $`H_i` form a finite generating set.
A group of permutations of $`X` acts faithfully on $`X`, so the bound on the orbits gives
finiteness.
:::

The first statement is a Lean instance, a fact that Lean finds automatically whenever it needs it;
its long name is generated from its statement. For two disks the library instead proves finite
generation directly from the two generators (Chapter 2).

:::lemma_ "compound_monotone" (lean := "TDCSG.orbit_subset_orbit_of_eqOn, TDCSG.finite_of_forall_orbit_subset") (uses := "compound_generators, ball_extension_facts, uniform_orbits")
Let $`G'` be a group of permutations of $`X`. Suppose each $`H_i` is generated by a set $`S_i`,
and for every $`s\in S_i` the extension $`\hat s` agrees on $`B_i` with some element of $`G'`.
Then every orbit of the compound symmetry group lies in the orbit of $`G'` of the same point.
Moreover, for any two groups $`G` and $`G'` of permutations of $`X`, with $`G` finitely
generated and $`G'` finite, if every orbit of $`G` lies in the orbit of $`G'` of the same point,
then $`G` is finite.
:::

:::proof "compound_monotone" (lean := "TDCSG.encard_orbit_le_natCard")
Every generator $`\hat s` maps the $`G'`-orbit $`O` of $`x` onto itself: on $`B_i` it acts as an
element of $`G'`, and off $`B_i` it is the identity. So the whole compound symmetry group maps
$`O` into itself, and the orbit of $`x` lies in $`O`. For the second statement, the orbits of a
finite group have at most as many points as the group, so the orbits of $`G` are uniformly
bounded and $`G` is finite.
:::

*Two disks.* The groups of the main theorem are compound symmetry groups in the plane $`X=\mathbb C` with two
balls, indexed by $`\iota=\{0,1\}`: the closed disks of radii $`r_1` and $`r_2` about $`-1` and
$`1`. Each disk carries the cyclic group generated by its rotation through a fixed angle about its
center, the restriction of a rotation of the plane that fixes the center. Chapter 2 gives the
details. The general facts above then say that points outside the two disks are fixed, that every
orbit stays inside the two disks apart from its base point, and that disjoint disks give the
product of two cyclic groups. The finiteness criterion is the form in which the finite half of the
main theorem is proved.

The two-disk groups were introduced by R. A. Hearn, W. Kretschmer, T. Rokicki, B. Streeter and
E. Vergo in [Two-Disk Compound Symmetry Groups](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12950). The formulation
for a family of balls in a pseudometric space is the one used by this library.
